arXiv:2509.12467v1 [cs.LG] 15 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Nonlocal Neural Tangent Kernels via Parameter-Space Interactions</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sriram Nagaraj
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Vishakh Hari
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>sriram.nagaraj.atl@gmail.com, vishakh.hari.08@gmail.com
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) framework has provided deep insights into the training dynamics of neural networks under gradient flow. However, it relies on the assumption that the network is differentiable with respect to its parameters, an assumption that breaks down when considering non-smooth target functions or parameterized models exhibiting non-differentiable behavior. In this work, we propose a Nonlocal Neural Tangent Kernel (NNTK) that replaces the local gradient with a nonlocal interaction-based approximation in parameter space. Nonlocal gradients are known to exist for a wider class of functions than the standard gradient. This allows NTK theory to be extended to nonsmooth functions, stochastic estimators, and broader families of models. We explore both fixed-kernel and attention-based formulations of this nonlocal operator. We illustrate the new formulation with numerical studies.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
        This work was independently carried out by the authors in their capacity as independent researchers. The views and content expressed in this article are solely those of the authors and do not reflect those of the Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland or the Federal Reserve System. </span></span></span></p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Understanding the training dynamics of neural networks is one of the central goals of theoretical deep learning. The Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK), introduced by Jacot et al. (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>), describes how the output of a neural network evolves during training under gradient flow. Indeed, NTK framework has emerged as a powerful tool for analyzing the training dynamics of wide neural networks. Specifically, the NTK approximates the training dynamics of a neural network by a process of linearizing the network output function <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x;\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x;\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> with input <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and with respect to its parameters <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. In the infinite-width limit, this approximation becomes exact.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, traditional NTK theory assumes infinitesimal parameter updates and linearized training dynamics, which can fall short in capturing nonlocal behavior of real-world networks. Specifically, we list some limitations of the traditional NTK formulation:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It assumes that the function <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x;\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x;\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is differentiable with respect to the parameters <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It treats parameter updates via infinitesimal local gradients, which may not capture more global or stochastic behavior.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It fails to handle target functions that are not differentiable (e.g., sign, sawtooth, or other piecewise functions). In practise, however, this issue is handled similar to that of autodifferentiation of non-differentiable functions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce a nonlocal version of the NTK—called the <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nonlocal Neural Tangent Kernel (NNTK)</span>—that generalizes the derivative operator by replacing it with a <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">nonlocal gradient</em> in parameter space. This operator integrates finite-difference information across neighborhoods of the parameter vector, weighted by an interaction kernel. Importantly, it is well-defined even when <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is not differentiable in <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We further propose a stochastic approximation of the Nonlocal NTK using Monte Carlo sampling over random parameter perturbations. To increase flexibility and adaptivity, we introduce an attention-based mechanism that assigns learned weights to each sampled perturbation. This leads to our main contribution: an attention-weighted Monte Carlo estimator of the Nonlocal NTK, with learnable number of samples <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background and Motivation</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a neural network <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x;\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x;\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the classical NTK is defined as:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(x,x^{\prime})=\nabla_{\theta}f(x;\theta)^{\top}\nabla_{\theta}f(x^{\prime};\theta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(x,x^{\prime})=\nabla_{\theta}f(x;\theta)^{\top}\nabla_{\theta}f(x^{\prime};\theta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This form of the NTK is obtained from gradient flow of the underlying function. Indeed, since <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x;\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x;\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is a neural network parameterized by <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> being a loss function, under gradient descent, the parameters evolve as:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d\theta}{dt}=-\nabla_{\theta}L(\theta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d\theta}{dt}=-\nabla_{\theta}L(\theta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The evolution of the output at a point <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the chain rule:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{df(x;\theta)}{dt}=\nabla_{\theta}f(x;\theta)^{\top}\frac{d\theta}{dt}=-\nabla_{\theta}f(x;\theta)^{\top}\nabla_{\theta}L(\theta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{df(x;\theta)}{dt}=\nabla_{\theta}f(x;\theta)^{\top}\frac{d\theta}{dt}=-\nabla_{\theta}f(x;\theta)^{\top}\nabla_{\theta}L(\theta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">In supervised learning, <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ell(f(x_{i};\theta),y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ell(f(x_{i};\theta),y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence, this yields</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}f(x;\theta_{t})=-\sum_{x^{\prime}}K(x,x^{\prime})\frac{\partial\ell}{\partial f(x^{\prime})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></munder><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}f(x;\theta_{t})=-\sum_{x^{\prime}}K(x,x^{\prime})\frac{\partial\ell}{\partial f(x^{\prime})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Need for Nonlocal Version</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The infinitesimal gradient assumption fails to account for finite and nonlocal parameter changes, and also assumes that both <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> are differentiable. In many practical scenarios, this is not true, and we must resort to nondifferentiable loss functions or network outputs. Second, even if the loss/network functions are differentiable, we may desire the inclusion of neighborhood values in a nonlocal fashion for averaging/smoothing purposes. This would necessitate the use of nonlocal version of the gradient operators to develop a NTK-like object in the nonlocal regime.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Nonlocal Parameter Derivatives</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further this discussion, we define a <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">nonlocal gradient operator</span> over parameter space. For a function <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x;\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x;\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, define:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}f(x;\theta):=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\frac{(f(x;\theta^{\prime})-f(x;\theta))(\theta^{\prime}-\theta)}{\|\theta^{\prime}-\theta\|^{2}}\rho_{\gamma}(\theta,\theta^{\prime})\,d\theta^{\prime}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}f(x;\theta):=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\frac{(f(x;\theta^{\prime})-f(x;\theta))(\theta^{\prime}-\theta)}{\|\theta^{\prime}-\theta\|^{2}}\rho_{\gamma}(\theta,\theta^{\prime})\,d\theta^{\prime},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is a symmetric, positive kernel that concentrates around <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., a Gaussian kernel):</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\gamma}(\theta,\theta^{\prime})=\frac{1}{Z}\exp\left(-\frac{\|\theta-\theta^{\prime}\|^{2}}{\gamma^{2}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>Z</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\gamma}(\theta,\theta^{\prime})=\frac{1}{Z}\exp\left(-\frac{\|\theta-\theta^{\prime}\|^{2}}{\gamma^{2}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This nonlocal operator is well-defined even when <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is not differentiable in <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, as it uses finite differences over a neighborhood. We refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> for the mathematical exposition of nonlocal operators used here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It can be interpreted as the conditional expectation of a <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">stochastic derivative</span>, where we sample perturbed parameters <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}\sim\rho_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}\sim\rho_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> and compute an average change in <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}f(x;\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\Theta^{\prime}\sim\rho_{\gamma}}[\frac{(f(x;\Theta^{\prime})-f(x;\theta))(\Theta^{\prime}-\theta)}{\|\Theta^{\prime}-\theta\|}]\," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}f(x;\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\Theta^{\prime}\sim\rho_{\gamma}}[\frac{(f(x;\Theta^{\prime})-f(x;\theta))(\Theta^{\prime}-\theta)}{\|\Theta^{\prime}-\theta\|}]\,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the limit <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, this operator converges (in a weak sense) to the classical derivative, under suitable regularity (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>).
Note that a directional version of the above formulation may also be specified where both the high dimensional integration in <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as the symmetric kernel requirement may be dropped (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>):</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\delta,\phi}f(\theta):=\int\frac{f(\theta^{\prime}+t\phi)-f(\theta)}{t}\rho_{\delta}(t)dt." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\delta,\phi}f(\theta):=\int\frac{f(\theta^{\prime}+t\phi)-f(\theta)}{t}\rho_{\delta}(t)dt.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This represents a nonlocal directional derivative at <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> in the direction of <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, and is a single dimensional nonlocal operator. We will consider the full nonlocal gradient <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}f(x;\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}f(x;\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> in subsequent discussions, though the directional alternative can be easily substituted in its place.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Nonlocal Gradient Perspective</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\delta}(\theta,\theta^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\delta}(\theta,\theta^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> be the kernel defining an interaction neighborhood in parameter space. Recall that the nonlocal gradient of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}f(x;\theta)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\frac{(f(x;\theta^{\prime})-f(x;\theta))(\theta^{\prime}-\theta)}{\|\theta^{\prime}-\theta\|^{2}}\rho_{\gamma}(\theta,\theta^{\prime})\,d\theta^{\prime}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}f(x;\theta)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\frac{(f(x;\theta^{\prime})-f(x;\theta))(\theta^{\prime}-\theta)}{\|\theta^{\prime}-\theta\|^{2}}\rho_{\gamma}(\theta,\theta^{\prime})\,d\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We define the Nonlocal Neural Tangent Kernel as:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\gamma}(x,x^{\prime}):=\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}f(x;\theta)^{\top}\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}f(x^{\prime};\theta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\gamma}(x,x^{\prime}):=\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}f(x;\theta)^{\top}\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}f(x^{\prime};\theta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This can be computed either analytically (when possible) or estimated using Monte Carlo sampling of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> from the kernel <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to the classical NTK, this kernel captures a broader spectrum of functional changes and is capable of operating in nondifferentiable or noisy settings. It generalizes the local gradient structure to a weighted average of directional perturbations.
Reformulating the nonlocal gradient as an expected value (as we saw earlier) motivates defining another form of the Nonlocal NTK:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\gamma}(x,x^{\prime}):=\mathbb{E}_{\Delta\sim\rho_{\gamma}}\left[\frac{f(x;\theta+\Delta)-f(x;\theta)}{\|\Delta\|}\cdot\frac{f(x^{\prime};\theta+\Delta)-f(x^{\prime};\theta)}{\|\Delta\|}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\gamma}(x,x^{\prime}):=\mathbb{E}_{\Delta\sim\rho_{\gamma}}\left[\frac{f(x;\theta+\Delta)-f(x;\theta)}{\|\Delta\|}\cdot\frac{f(x^{\prime};\theta+\Delta)-f(x^{\prime};\theta)}{\|\Delta\|}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here, we assume a sacalar valued function <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x,\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x,\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed within the expectation. Note that instead of a factor <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\Delta}{\|\Delta\|^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\Delta}{\|\Delta\|^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\|\Delta\|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\|\Delta\|}</annotation></semantics></math> in each factor of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\gamma}(x,x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\gamma}(x,x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. This has the same scaling and asymptotic features, and indeed, had we defined</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\gamma}(x,x^{\prime}):=\mathbb{E}_{\Delta\sim\rho_{\gamma}}\left[\frac{(f(x;\theta+\Delta)-f(x;\theta))\Delta^{\top}}{\|\Delta\|^{2}}\cdot\frac{(f(x^{\prime};\theta+\Delta)-f(x^{\prime};\theta))\Delta}{\|\Delta\|^{2}}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\gamma}(x,x^{\prime}):=\mathbb{E}_{\Delta\sim\rho_{\gamma}}\left[\frac{(f(x;\theta+\Delta)-f(x;\theta))\Delta^{\top}}{\|\Delta\|^{2}}\cdot\frac{(f(x^{\prime};\theta+\Delta)-f(x^{\prime};\theta))\Delta}{\|\Delta\|^{2}}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">we would have obtained the same expression.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Monte Carlo Approximation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can go one step further, and approximate the Nonlocal NTK using <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> random perturbations <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Delta_{k}\}_{k=1}^{N}\sim\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Delta_{k}\}_{k=1}^{N}\sim\rho</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{K}(x,x^{\prime})=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^{N}\frac{\delta_{k}(x)\cdot\delta_{k}(x^{\prime})}{\|\Delta_{k}\|^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{K}(x,x^{\prime})=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^{N}\frac{\delta_{k}(x)\cdot\delta_{k}(x^{\prime})}{\|\Delta_{k}\|^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}(x):=f(x;\theta+\Delta_{k})-f(x;\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}(x):=f(x;\theta+\Delta_{k})-f(x;\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>. This estimator can be computed without access to gradients, making it amenable to black-box settings, in the sense of zeroth-order methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Attention-Based Weighting</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of a fixed kernel <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can use an <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">attention mechanism</span> to adaptively weight the importance of each <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, for a fixed collection of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> acting as “anchors”, we can define:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\gamma}^{\text{att}}(\theta,\theta^{\prime})=\frac{\exp\left(-\frac{\|f(x;\theta)-f(x;\theta^{\prime})\|^{2}}{\gamma^{2}}\right)}{\sum_{j}\exp\left(-\frac{\|f(x;\theta)-f(x;\theta_{j})\|^{2}}{\gamma^{2}}\right)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>γ</mi><mtext>att</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\gamma}^{\text{att}}(\theta,\theta^{\prime})=\frac{\exp\left(-\frac{\|f(x;\theta)-f(x;\theta^{\prime})\|^{2}}{\gamma^{2}}\right)}{\sum_{j}\exp\left(-\frac{\|f(x;\theta)-f(x;\theta_{j})\|^{2}}{\gamma^{2}}\right)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which gives higher weight to parameter perturbations that result in similar outputs. This encourages the model to attend to directions that preserve output geometry.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Nonlocal Gradient Flow Dynamics</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under nonlocal dynamics, the parameter update becomes:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d\theta}{dt}=-\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}L(\theta)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d\theta}{dt}=-\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}L(\theta),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which is the nonlocal analog of gradient flow. When <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is not differentiable, the operator <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}L</annotation></semantics></math> still exists under mild assumptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This formulation can also be interpreted as a smoothed version of training dynamics that respects a neighborhood in parameter space, leading to potentially more stable training and better generalization.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We illustrate the foregoing discussion with numerical examples. We first compare the heatmap of the various NTKs: standard, attention based and Monte Carlo with perturbations drawn from uniform/Gaussian distributions. We use challenging nonsmooth target functions <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> such as:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=\text{sign}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=\text{sign}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=x-\lfloor x\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=x-\lfloor x\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> (sawtooth)</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">These functions are not differentiable, so a standard NTK would struggle to capture their behavior. We then show how the spectrum of the NTK i.e., the spectrum of the Kernel matrices <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(x_{i},x_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(x_{i},x_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> evolves. Finally, we consider the kernel alignment:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{alignment}(K,y)=\frac{y^{\top}Ky}{\|K\|_{F}\cdot\|y\|^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>alignment</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{alignment}(K,y)=\frac{y^{\top}Ky}{\|K\|_{F}\cdot\|y\|^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and plot this for both the standard and nonlocal NTK in the case of the sign function. All these numerical examples are provided in the appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced a framework for extending NTK to nonsmooth regimes via nonlocal derivatives in parameter space. This leads to a generalized NTK (NNTK) that is both theoretically motivated and empirically powerful. We illustrated our ideas using numerical examples of nonsmooth network functions, and compared the spectrum of NTK matrices in the standard vs. nonlocal regimes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Future work includes:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theoretical convergence guarantees for NNTK.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extension to convolutional networks and Transformers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Applications to adversarial robustness and Bayesian models.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
